Once upon a time, a Gnome Warlock named Thaddeus was questing through the treacherous terrain of Stranglethorn Vale. He had a Succubus as his pet, a sleek and powerful creature that could stealth, cc, and deal damage. Thaddeus was confident in his Succubus’s abilities, especially for solo leveling and questing. But as he ventured deeper into the wilds, he encountered a group of hostile goblins. The Succubus used her Lesser Invisibility to evade, but the goblins were too numerous. Thaddeus needed a way to hold them off. He summoned his Voidwalker, who immediately began taunting the goblins and generating threat. The Voidwalker’s Sacrifice shield protected Thaddeus while he fought back. Though the Succubus was strong, the Voidwalker’s crowd control and threat generation made the difference in this dangerous encounter. Thaddeus realized that while the Succubus was excellent for stealth and damage, the Voidwalker was better for group situations and survival. So, even though the Succubus is often the better choice, the Voidwalker is more commonly seen in classic WoW because of its utility in group settings and survival. [end of text]


